    <header style="margin-bottom: 36px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(61,43,31,0.1); padding-bottom: 24px;">
      <p style="text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--copper); margin-bottom: 12px;">Kitchen Cabinet Care Guide</p>
      <h1 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(30px, 4vw, 44px); color: var(--walnut); line-height: 1.25; margin-bottom: 16px;">
        Tips for Cleaning Kitchen Cabinets: A Practical Guide
      </h1>
      <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--warm-gray); margin-bottom: 0;">Published by Choice Kitchen Cabinet &bull; First published 16 May 2024 &bull; Updated 4 November 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p style="font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 24px;">
      Spills, cooking splatters and greasy fingerprints build up quickly in a kitchen that is used every day. Left alone, that film of grease and dust dulls the finish and becomes harder to remove. The good news is that routine cleaning of kitchen cabinets is simple, and it helps the cabinets look better for longer.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      This guide covers a basic weekly routine, how to deep clean the inside of your cabinets, extra care for different cabinet materials, and how often to clean each area. It is general household advice, not a substitute for the care instructions that come with your own cabinets or products.
    </p>

    <h2 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(24px, 2.5vw, 30px); color: var(--walnut); margin: 36px 0 16px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(61,43,31,0.08); padding-top: 24px;">
      Before You Start: Three Quick Rules
    </h2>
    <ul style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-left: 24px; color: var(--text-body); line-height: 1.8;">
      <li><strong>Check the care instructions first.</strong> If you have a manufacturer's or installer's care sheet for your cabinets, follow it ahead of any general advice here.</li>
      <li><strong>Test in a hidden spot.</strong> Try any new cleaner on a small, inconspicuous area, such as the inside edge of a door, and wait for it to dry before cleaning the whole surface.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep cloths damp, not wet.</strong> Excess water running into edges, joints and hinges is the most common cause of swelling and peeling on board-based cabinets.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(24px, 2.5vw, 30px); color: var(--walnut); margin: 36px 0 16px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(61,43,31,0.08); padding-top: 24px;">
      Regular Cleaning of Cabinet Doors and Fronts
    </h2>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 12px;">
      These steps suit most finished cabinet surfaces, including laminate, melamine, painted and sealed wood:
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-left: 24px; color: var(--text-body); line-height: 1.8;">
      <li><strong>Dust first.</strong> Wipe the surface with a dry microfiber cloth so loose dust is not smeared into a paste when you add moisture.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose a gentle cleaner.</strong> A few drops of mild dish soap in warm water works for most routine grime. A grease-cutting household cleaner can be used if it is suitable for your cabinet finish.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply to the cloth, not the cabinet.</strong> Wring the cloth out well so it is only damp.</li>
      <li><strong>Wipe along the grain or surface direction.</strong> Clean both faces of each door, and pay extra attention to the area around handles and the edges nearest the stove, where grease collects.</li>
      <li><strong>Deal with stubborn spots gently.</strong> For dried-on grime, hold a damp, soapy cloth against the spot for a minute to soften it, then wipe. Avoid scouring pads and abrasive powders, which can scratch most finishes.</li>
      <li><strong>Rinse.</strong> Wipe again with a cloth dampened with clean water to remove soap residue, which otherwise attracts dust.</li>
      <li><strong>Dry and buff.</strong> Dry straight away with a clean microfiber cloth. If you use a cabinet polish or cream on finished wood, follow the label directions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(24px, 2.5vw, 30px); color: var(--walnut); margin: 36px 0 16px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(61,43,31,0.08); padding-top: 24px;">
      Cleaning Notes by Cabinet Material
    </h2>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Different finishes tolerate different treatment. The points below are general guidelines; your product's own instructions always come first.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 21px; color: var(--walnut); margin: 24px 0 10px;">Laminate and melamine</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      These surfaces are generally easy to wipe down with mild soapy water. Dry them promptly, especially along door edges and near the sink, because water that seeps into the board core or edging can cause lifting over time. Avoid abrasive pads, which can dull the surface.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 21px; color: var(--walnut); margin: 24px 0 10px;">Wood and wood veneer</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Use a barely damp cloth and dry immediately. Avoid soaking the surface, steam cleaners and strong solvents, which can damage the finish. Wipe in the direction of the grain, and use a polish only if it is recommended for your finish.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 21px; color: var(--walnut); margin: 24px 0 10px;">Painted and lacquered finishes</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Painted or lacquered doors can mark if rubbed hard. Use a soft cloth and mild soap, press lightly, and avoid scrubbing glossy surfaces in circles, as this can leave visible swirl marks. Check any cleaner on a hidden area first.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 21px; color: var(--walnut); margin: 24px 0 10px;">Glass doors and glass panels</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Use a glass cleaner or a little white vinegar diluted in water on a cloth, and keep the liquid off the surrounding cabinet frame. Dry with a clean microfiber cloth to avoid streaks.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 21px; color: var(--walnut); margin: 24px 0 10px;">Aluminium frames and profiles</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Wipe with a soft cloth and mild soapy water, then dry. Avoid abrasive pads and strong acidic or alkaline cleaners, which can mark anodised or coated aluminium.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 21px; color: var(--walnut); margin: 24px 0 10px;">Handles, knobs and hinges</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Handles collect the most fingerprints. Wipe them with a damp soapy cloth and dry them. Use a soft toothbrush for grooves and screw heads. Keep cleaner away from hinges and sliding runners, and wipe off any moisture around them.
    </p>

    <h2 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(24px, 2.5vw, 30px); color: var(--walnut); margin: 36px 0 16px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(61,43,31,0.08); padding-top: 24px;">
      Deep Cleaning the Inside of Cabinets and Drawers
    </h2>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 12px;">
      Every few months, empty and clean the inside of your cabinets to remove crumbs, dust and sticky rings from bottles.
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-left: 24px; color: var(--text-body); line-height: 1.8;">
      <li><strong>Empty in sections.</strong> Start with the upper cabinets and work down, emptying one cabinet or drawer at a time so the job stays manageable.</li>
      <li><strong>Vacuum or brush out loose debris</strong> from shelves, corners and drawer tracks.</li>
      <li><strong>Wipe the surfaces</strong> with a damp microfiber cloth and mild soapy water, including shelves and the back of the doors.</li>
      <li><strong>Detail the edges.</strong> Use a small brush or soft toothbrush on grooves, hardware and trim where dust and grease settle.</li>
      <li><strong>Dry completely</strong> and leave doors open for a short while before putting items back, so no moisture is trapped inside.</li>
    </ol>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 24px;">
      This is also a good time to check hinges and handles for looseness and to sort out items you no longer use. For ideas on arranging what goes back in, see our guide to <a href="/how-to-organize-kitchen-cabinets/" style="color: var(--copper); font-weight: 600;">how to organize kitchen cabinets</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(24px, 2.5vw, 30px); color: var(--walnut); margin: 36px 0 16px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(61,43,31,0.08); padding-top: 24px;">
      How Often Should You Clean Kitchen Cabinets?
    </h2>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 12px;">
      A simple schedule is easier to keep than a perfect one. Adjust it to how often you cook, especially if you do a lot of frying:
    </p>
    <ul style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-left: 24px; color: var(--text-body); line-height: 1.8;">
      <li><strong>As it happens:</strong> wipe up spills and splatters promptly with a damp cloth, before they dry on.</li>
      <li><strong>Weekly:</strong> a quick wipe of door fronts, handles and the areas around the stove and sink.</li>
      <li><strong>Every 3 to 6 months:</strong> a deeper clean of the inside of cabinets and drawers, plus the top of the cabinets if they are exposed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(24px, 2.5vw, 30px); color: var(--walnut); margin: 36px 0 16px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(61,43,31,0.08); padding-top: 24px;">
      Habits That Help Cabinets Stay Clean and Last Longer
    </h2>
    <ul style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-left: 24px; color: var(--text-body); line-height: 1.8;">
      <li><strong>Use shelf liners</strong> in cabinets where spills are likely, such as under the sink and in the pantry. Liners are quick to wipe or replace.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid harsh chemicals.</strong> Bleach, oven cleaners and strong solvents can damage finishes. Start with the mildest option that works.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the extractor hood or open a window when frying.</strong> Capturing cooking vapour at the source means less grease settles on nearby cabinet doors.</li>
      <li><strong>Mind the humidity.</strong> In a humid climate, wipe away moisture and avoid putting wet items into closed cabinets, so damp does not linger inside.</li>
      <li><strong>Look for early damage.</strong> Peeling edges, swelling near the sink or loose hinges are easier to fix if caught early.</li>
      <li><strong>Wipe handles more often,</strong> since they are the most-touched part of the cabinet.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 20px;">
      If you are planning new cabinets, it also helps to decide how much upkeep you are comfortable with, because finishes and materials differ in how easy they are to keep clean. Measuring your space accurately is a good first step; our guide to <a href="/calculating-kitchen-cabinet-space/" style="color: var(--copper); font-weight: 600;">calculating kitchen cabinet space</a> explains how.
    </p>
